rt-title = Quick in the room, patient in the background.
rt-lede = The central agent holds the conversation with a fast model and a few direct tools. Anything long goes to an executor as a durable task, and the conversation carries on.
rt-lane-you = You
rt-lane-agent = Agent
rt-lane-executor = Executor
rt-you-1 = Turn on the living room. And plan our weekend in the mountains.
rt-you-2 = mm-hm
rt-you-3 = wait, actually…
rt-agent-1 = Done, it’s on. I’m on the weekend, carry on.
rt-agent-2 = It’s ready: two options, sent to your phone.
rt-tool = Home Assistant · light on · checked
rt-task = task · research, two options
rt-ack = an acknowledgement, not an interruption
rt-cut = an interruption: it stops speaking
rt-1 = Direct or delegated
rt-1-text = Frequent and short (the home, memory, timers, quick lookups) is a direct tool, answered in the turn. Long and complex is delegated: acknowledged first, followed, announced once.
rt-2 = Streaming end to end
rt-2-text = Partial transcripts, end-of-turn detection, and a reply spoken while it is still being written. The execution harness never sits in the conversation loop.
rt-3 = It knows when to stop
rt-3-text = A “mm-hm” doesn’t stop it; “wait” does. What was cut is written to the journal as cut, so the agent knows what you heard.
rt-4 = Cancelling is confirmed
rt-4-text = Asking to cancel is never mistaken for having cancelled: a task is cancelled only once the executor confirms it stopped.
rt-5 = Durable promises
rt-5-text = Tasks and watches live in the journal. They survive restarts and model changes, and deliver once, to the right person.
rt-6 = Failure said aloud
rt-6-text = When a provider fails or stalls, it says so instead of going quiet. Without an optional capability it carries on with less; without a required one it stops at start and says why.
